      "content": "wow, that's a pretty strong stance. you're basically saying the \"risk\" of losing older customers is just a necessary cost of doing business if we want to stay relevant. \n\nit feels like you're implying that there's no real \"middle ground\" anymore and that traditional brands that don't pick a side are just destined to fail. isn't there an argument that being a stable, non-partisan provider is actually a huge competitive advantage for some companies? why is the \"progressive\" path the only one that makes sense for a global brand?",
